---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import PageHero from "../../components/PageHero.astro";
import PhotoSlot from "../../components/PhotoSlot.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Storytelling as Medicine"
  description="An essay by Robert Sanchez, founder of The Jentosy Project, on why storytelling is central to how the organization approaches kidney health equity."
>
  <PageHero
    eyebrow="An Essay by Robert Sanchez"
    heading="Storytelling as Medicine"
    lede="Why lived experience belongs alongside clinical data — and why every story has the power to save a life."
  />

  <section class="on-light section">
    <div class="container narrow essay">
      <p class="reveal">
        Kidney disease is often measured in laboratory values, imaging studies, and clinical
        outcomes. Physicians monitor creatinine levels, estimated glomerular filtration rate
        (eGFR), proteinuria, dialysis adequacy, and transplant survival. These measurements are
        essential, but they tell only part of the story. They cannot capture the fear of hearing
        the words, <em>&ldquo;Your kidneys are failing.&rdquo;</em> They cannot explain the
        isolation of sitting in a dialysis chair three days a week, the uncertainty of waiting for
        a transplant, or the resilience required to rebuild a life after surgery.
      </p>

      <p class="pull reveal" style="--reveal-delay:0.06s">
        At The Jentosy Project, we believe that storytelling is medicine.
      </p>

      <p class="reveal">
        Stories have the power to heal in ways data alone cannot. They reduce fear by replacing
        uncertainty with understanding. They create hope by showing patients that someone else has
        walked the same difficult path and emerged on the other side. They build trust in
        healthcare systems that have often failed underserved communities. Most importantly, they
        remind people that they are more than a diagnosis — they are individuals with families,
        dreams, cultures, histories, and futures worth fighting for.
      </p>

      <p class="reveal">
        <strong>As a two-time kidney transplant recipient, I know this firsthand.</strong>
      </p>

      <figure class="essay-figure reveal">
        <img
          class="essay-photo"
          src="/images/founder-podium.webp"
          alt="Robert Sanchez speaking at a podium about kidney disease and health equity"
        />
        <figcaption>Robert speaking publicly about kidney disease and health equity.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p class="reveal">
        When I was diagnosed with FSGS and began dialysis, I searched desperately for answers. I
        wanted someone to explain not only what was happening to my body, but what was happening
        to my life. The medical information I found was often technical, fragmented, and
        difficult to relate to. What ultimately changed my perspective were the stories of people
        who had lived through kidney disease before me. Their experiences gave language to
        emotions I could not express and showed me that survival was possible. Those stories
        transformed fear into purpose and inspired me to dedicate my life to kidney health
        advocacy.
      </p>

      <p class="reveal">
        Storytelling has long been recognized as a powerful tool in medicine. Patients who hear
        stories from peers often report reduced anxiety, greater confidence in treatment
        decisions, improved engagement with healthcare providers, and a stronger sense of
        belonging. For communities that have experienced generations of healthcare inequities and
        mistrust, stories frequently become the bridge that evidence alone cannot build.
      </p>

      <p class="reveal">This is particularly important in kidney disease.</p>

      <p class="reveal">
        Kidney disease disproportionately affects Black, Hispanic, Indigenous, and other
        historically underserved communities. These disparities are not simply biological; they
        are shaped by where people live, the environments they grow up in, access to healthy
        food, transportation, quality healthcare, economic opportunity, education, and countless
        other social determinants of health. Statistics reveal these inequities, but stories
        explain them. They provide context, humanity, and understanding behind the numbers.
      </p>

      <p class="reveal">That philosophy is central to <strong>The Jentosy Project</strong>.</p>

      <p class="reveal">
        Our mission is to advance health equity by integrating data, geography, and patient
        storytelling to address disparities in kidney disease. We believe that lived experience is
        a form of evidence. Every patient's journey contributes knowledge that can improve
        research, strengthen public policy, inform clinical practice, and empower future patients.
        Storytelling is not separate from science — it complements it by revealing what
        traditional datasets cannot.
      </p>

      <p class="reveal">
        This philosophy is embodied in our flagship initiative,
        <strong>Where We Live &mdash; The Rare Renal Equity Project (REP)</strong>. REP combines
        geographic data, social determinants of health, and lived patient experiences to
        illustrate how place influences kidney health. Maps can identify neighborhoods with higher
        disease burden, fewer transplant referrals, or limited access to healthy food. Patient
        stories reveal what those conditions feel like, how they shape daily life, and why
        structural inequities matter. Together, data and narrative create a fuller understanding
        than either could provide alone.
      </p>

      <PhotoSlot
        src="/images/where-we-live-rep.jpg"
        alt="A Where We Live — Rare Renal Equity Project community mapping session"
        ratio="16 / 9"
      />

      <p class="reveal">Our goal extends beyond awareness.</p>

      <p class="reveal">
        We want every story to become a source of social capital — a resource that helps patients
        navigate the healthcare system more effectively. Stories teach patients which questions to
        ask, what resources exist, how to advocate for themselves, and where to find hope during
        difficult moments. They also educate clinicians, researchers, policymakers, and health
        systems by centering the voices of those living with kidney disease every day.
      </p>

      <p class="reveal">
        When patients recognize themselves in another person's experience, they become less
        isolated. When clinicians hear authentic patient narratives, they become more empathetic
        and better equipped to provide person-centered care. When researchers understand the
        lived realities behind disparities, they ask better questions. When policymakers hear both
        the data and the stories, they are more likely to pursue meaningful structural change.
      </p>

      <p class="pull reveal">
        This is why storytelling is not simply communication &mdash; it is intervention.
      </p>

      <p class="reveal">
        It improves health literacy. It strengthens trust. It encourages earlier screening and
        treatment. It increases participation in research. It supports shared decision-making. It
        inspires living kidney donation. It reduces stigma. It builds community. And perhaps most
        importantly, it reminds every patient that they are not alone.
      </p>

      <p class="reveal">
        At The Jentosy Project, we envision a future where every patient story has the power to
        improve another person's outcome. Where narrative and science work together to reshape
        healthcare. Where geography, data, and lived experience reveal not only where disparities
        exist, but how we can eliminate them.
      </p>

      <p class="essay-closer reveal">
        Because healing begins with being seen.<br />
        And every story has the power to save a life.
      </p>

      <div class="essay-byline reveal">
        <p class="essay-byline-name">Robert Sanchez</p>
        <p class="essay-byline-title">Founder &amp; CEO, The Jentosy Project</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="on-light section-tight" style="border-top:1px solid var(--line);">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <a href="/about/team/" class="btn btn-steel">Meet Robert &rarr;</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .narrow {
    max-width: 700px;
  }

  .essay p {
    color: var(--fg-soft);
  }

  .essay-figure {
    margin: var(--space-6) 0;
  }

  .essay-photo {
    width: 100%;
    aspect-ratio: 4 / 3;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: center 25%;
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
  }

  .essay-figure figcaption {
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
    font-size: 12.5px;
    color: var(--fg-muted);
  }

  .essay-closer {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-style: italic;
    font-weight: 500;
    font-size: clamp(20px, 2.4vw, 26px);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--fg);
    margin: var(--space-7) 0;
  }

  .essay-byline {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
    padding-top: var(--space-4);
    margin-top: var(--space-6);
  }

  .essay-byline-name {
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
    font-size: 16px;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .essay-byline-title {
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--fg-muted);
    margin: var(--space-1) 0 0;
  }
</style>
